1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,800
Start with one child, and one fever.

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:08,200
Her dengue case is written down at her home address.

3
00:00:08,300 --> 00:00:15,300
But the mosquito that carries dengue bites by day, at school, at the market, at work.

4
00:00:15,500 --> 00:00:20,300
So where was she bitten, and where should the brigade clean?

5
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:22,400
Zoom out.

6
00:00:22,600 --> 00:00:27,400
In September 2026, Albay declared a state of calamity over dengue.

7
00:00:27,600 --> 00:00:32,800
Cases rose eighty-four percent from the year before, and fourteen people died.

8
00:00:32,900 --> 00:00:38,800
Sustainable Development Goal 3 asks us to end such epidemics, and to warn early.

9
00:00:39,500 --> 00:00:41,400
Now, the response.

10
00:00:41,500 --> 00:00:47,400
Brigades clean and spray where cases live, because that is what the reports show.

11
00:00:47,500 --> 00:00:53,400
But if the mosquitoes breed at a school or a market, the source survives.

12
00:00:53,500 --> 00:00:58,700
And when the wards fill up, the outbreak is already weeks old.

13
00:00:59,500 --> 00:01:03,900
You may be wondering: how does MCS come into play?

14
00:01:04,200 --> 00:01:08,400
Mathematics turns where people spend the day into a network.

15
00:01:08,500 --> 00:01:13,100
Dengue's reproduction number becomes the largest eigenvalue of one symmetric matrix.

16
00:01:13,500 --> 00:01:18,500
Computing traces cases from home addresses back to the places of infection.

17
00:01:18,600 --> 00:01:26,500
In the test network, homes point to B2, but the school and market in B1 carry the most transmission.

18
00:01:26,900 --> 00:01:32,800
Optimisation finds the cheapest brigade plan that brings R-naught below one, and certifies it.

19
00:01:33,700 --> 00:01:39,200
And a CUSUM alarm adds up small weekly rises, so warnings come sooner.

20
00:01:39,500 --> 00:01:42,300
Twelve results, proven, then verified.

21
00:01:44,300 --> 00:01:47,500
BANTA-Y will not kill a single mosquito.

22
00:01:47,600 --> 00:01:55,500
But once cases are reported, it will tell the brigade where to go first, and how much is enough.

23
00:01:57,600 --> 00:02:01,100
An address tells you where the sick live.

24
00:02:01,200 --> 00:02:04,700
Where people go tells you where dengue goes.

